  "anomaly_detected": false,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [],
  "summary": "The drawing faithfully reproduces the reference design described in the specification: every part value, note and annotation matches (XT60-M input, 10 mΩ shunt + INA219 at 0x40, MIC26903 buck with 15 µH inductor and 6k8/1k3 divider giving ~5 V, LD39200PU33R 3.3 V LDO, STM32H753VITx FCC + STM32F405RGTx NAVC, BMI088/IIS2MDCTR/MPRLS/TMP100/MAX-8Q/24CW1280T/W25N01GVZEIG/PCA9685PW, 25 MHz ABM8G crystals, TDD01H0SB1R boot switches, dual CH330), and the only emphatic annotation ('RESET AND INTERRUPT PINS ARE MIXED UP!!!!') is the documented V1 bug, not a deviation. The single apparent numeric mismatch — the PCA9685 being strapped to I2C 0x41 (A0 tied to +3.3 V) whereas the spec table lists 0x40 — is the electrically required resolution of an address clash with the INA219 (0x40) that shares the same SCL/SDA net pair (NET0088/NET0170); the schematic's own 'I2C address: 0x41' note confirms 0x41 as the intended strap, so this is a spec-table typo rather than a board anomaly. No confirmed deviation from intended behaviour was found."
}

